# UC-001 · Create user

> Parent spec. Fixes the boundary and the names; **doesn't** detail any layer.
> Per-layer detail lives in this folder's partials, each with its own owner.

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Identifier | `UC-001-create-user` |
| Date | 2026-09-07 |
| Trigger | user creation, over HTTP — inbound REST adapter. Ends in exactly one of: `inbound REST adapter` · `inbound GraphQL adapter` · `inbound REST and GraphQL adapters` · `inbound messaging adapter` · `inbound job adapter` — that phrase decides which inbound design steps run. **The exact path and verb belong to `rest-api-architect`, in `30-rest.md`; a GraphQL operation's shape to `graphql-api-architect`, in `31-graphql.md`** |
| Access | only callers with the `USER_ADMIN` role — a business fact. Mechanism, annotation and status belong to `security-architect`, in `32-seguranca.md`. One of: public · authenticated · role or group (named) · owner (and who may override). Required whenever the trigger is HTTP — REST, GraphQL, or both |
| Side effects | **1** — `INSERT` into `users` |
| Storage | `User` → relational — chosen by: request ("store the user in the existing Postgres"). One entry per aggregate the case writes or reads as its source, each `relational` or `document (MongoDB)` — `none` when it reads and writes no stored aggregate — with who chose: `request` (stated, or inferred without ambiguity — the aggregate already lives there) or `answer` (one factual question). Never weighed here: no criteria, no recommendation. Tables, collections and the relational engine belong to `persistence-architect`, in `20-persistencia.md` |
| Search index | none. When the request names a search over the data: `<aggregate> → Elasticsearch projection` or `→ OpenSearch projection`, with who chose. A search index is always a projection of a `Storage` aggregate, never where it lives. Mapping, query and indexer belong to `search-architect`, in `23-busca.md` |
| Audit | none — the request asks nothing about who changed the data. When it does, per aggregate the case writes: `<aggregate> → stamps` (when and by whom created and last changed) or `→ history` (every earlier version readable, with who and when), with who chose: `request`, or `answer` (one factual question, only when the request leaves open whether earlier values must be readable). A missing row reads as `none`. Columns, history tables, the actor and the retention belong to `persistence-architect`, in `20-persistencia.md` |
| Admission | none — the request states no limit. When it does, in its own numbers: `quota <N> per <window> per caller` and/or `cap <N> at the same time`, with who chose: `request`, or `answer` (one factual question, only for a missing window or caller). Only for an HTTP trigger; a missing row reads as `none`. Where the quota is enforced, the caller's key and the rejection belong to `rest-api-architect` (`30-rest.md` § 8) or `graphql-api-architect` (`31-graphql.md` § 6) |
| External calls | none — no side effect reaches a system this service does not own. When one does, one line per system: what it is and what the case asks of it, documentation link, how it authenticates this service (as itself · on behalf of the user · static key · username and password · client certificate · signed requests · none) and who owns the credential, idempotency key accepted or not, tolerance when it is down, and what an unknown write result means. Client, timeout and retry belong to `http-client-architect`, in `28-cliente-http.md` |
| Transaction | opens at the inbound port, closes before the response |
| Repetition | repeating the request with the same email must **not** create a second user (business fact). The mechanism — `Idempotency-Key` or none — is delegated to `rest-api-architect` |
| Concurrency | the `UNIQUE(email)` constraint is the arbiter; no application-level lock |
| Freshness | none — a create returns what it just wrote. For a read: how old an answer may be when served, in business words ("a balance may be up to 30 seconds old"), or `none` when every read must see the last write. A duration here is what makes `cache-architect` run; whether a cache exists, its provider, expiry and keys belong to it, in `22-cache.md`. With a `Search index`, the same duration is the whole budget: the projection's lag comes out of it first (`23-busca.md` § 6), and a cache gets only the remainder |
| Active blueprint | `hexagonal` |

## Trigger, payload, and response

**In**

| Field | Type | Required | Rule |
|---|---|---|---|
| `email` | string | ✅ | valid format, unique in the system |
| `name` | string | ✅ | 2 to 120 characters |
| `department` | string | ➖ | one of `Department`'s values |

**Out**

Situations, not statuses. The HTTP code for each is `rest-api-architect`'s decision —
this spec fixing it was what produced divergence with `30-rest.md`, which then had to
correct it in prose.

| Situation | Result |
|---|---|
| created | reference to the created resource + `id`, `email`, `name` |
| malformed payload | rejected without touching the domain |
| invalid email or name | rejected by the aggregate's invariants |
| email already in use | conflict with existing state |

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    trigger((user creation)) --> shape{"payload shape valid?"}
    shape -->|no| malformed(["malformed payload"])
    shape -->|yes| invariants{"email and name valid?"}
    invariants -->|no| invalid(["invalid email or name"])
    invariants -->|yes| unique{"email already in use?"}
    unique -->|yes| conflict(["email already in use"])
    unique -->|no| created(["created"])
```

One end node per situation above, and the checks in the order `## Flow` makes them — the
shape in the adapter, the invariants when the aggregate is built, uniqueness at the outbound
port — `@.claude/rules/spec-diagrams.md`.

## Flow

1. The inbound adapter receives the request and validates the payload's **shape**.
2. Translates it into the domain command `CreateUserCommand`.
3. The inbound port `CreateUserUseCase` executes: builds the `User` aggregate, which
   validates its invariants in the constructor.
4. The outbound port `UserRepository` persists it.
5. The adapter translates the returned aggregate into the response.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor caller
    participant adapter as inbound adapter
    participant CreateUserUseCase
    participant User
    participant UserRepository
    caller->>adapter: request, its shape validated
    adapter->>CreateUserUseCase: CreateUserCommand
    CreateUserUseCase->>User: builds it, invariants in the constructor
    CreateUserUseCase->>UserRepository: persists User
    Note right of UserRepository: INSERT into users
    adapter-->>caller: response from the returned aggregate
```

Nothing in this flow names annotations, columns, or framework types — that belongs to
the partials. The diagram is a view of the numbered list, one message per step, and adds
nothing to it — `@.claude/rules/spec-diagrams.md`.

