Paths derived from the active blueprint's `packages.map`. If the blueprint changes,
this table is what gets rewritten — never the flow above. The persistence path carries
one extra level, the aggregate's own subpackage (`…/persistence/user/`), whatever the
blueprint calls the layer — `@.claude/rules/persistence.md` § Boundary. The use-case
paths carry one too, the aggregate the use case writes (`…/port/in/user/`,
`…/service/user/`, `…/usecase/user/`), whenever the active blueprint's naming convention
groups use cases by aggregate — that convention owns the rule; a blueprint whose
convention says nothing about grouping keeps the flat path.

A domain field's row names the field and what it holds; the **shape stays open** — value
object or `enum` is `domain-modeling`'s call, by `@.claude/rules/value-objects.md`, a rule
this spec doesn't read. Writing `value object` here for a closed set produces a divergence
this table guarantees and nobody learns from.

## Invariants

| Rule | Guaranteed by | Who violates it |
|---|---|---|
| `email` has a valid format | `Email`'s constructor | caller with an arbitrary string |
| `email` is unique in the system | `UNIQUE` constraint + check at the outbound port | race between two requests |
| `name` is 2 to 120 characters | `User`'s constructor | overly long payload |

Invariants live in the aggregate, never in the caller —
`@.claude/rules/architecture-ddd.md`.

## Errors

| Situation | Kind |
|---|---|
| email already in use | conflict with existing state |
| malformed email | validation |
| name outside 2 to 120 characters | validation |
| required field missing | shape — rejected in the adapter, never reaches the domain |

Situations and kinds, never exception names or statuses. The exception class and
`errorCode` belong to `10-dominio.md` (`@.claude/rules/error-handling.md`); the HTTP
mapping belongs to `30-rest.md` — the use case is transport-agnostic.

