| unit | use case: propagates the email-in-use conflict | `test-architect` |
| slice | controller: created, malformed payload, and email in use | `test-architect` |
| integration | `UserRepositoryAdapter` against a real database | `test-architect` |

## Out of scope for this use case

Written down so nobody merges it back in by accident:

- Sending a welcome email → **UC-002**, reactive to the `UserCreated` event. Out
  because SMTP isn't transactional with the `INSERT`.
- Updating and deleting a user → their own use cases, their own triggers.
- Authentication and token issuance → outside this use case's domain.

## Impact on approved use cases

none — UC-001 is the first case.

Approved specs (`status: approved` or `implemented`) are read-only. When this case needs
a change in one — a new column, a promoted exception — it's written here instead, one
row per change, and the executor applies it in code. The old spec is never edited. Each
partial carries this same section for the changes its owner decides — an exception
promoted in `10-dominio.md`, a column added in `20-persistencia.md` — and consolidation
collects them all:

`| Approved case | Change | Why | Satisfied by |` — e.g. `| UC-001-create-user | the `User` aggregate gains a rename operation | this case renames; UC-001 only created | this case |`

The fourth column is only ever empty when the change adds **no** precondition. A row that
makes an earlier case require a state — a status, a flag, a related record — says which use
case produces that state: an approved `UC-NNN`, this case itself, or a named backlog case. In
the last situation the row spells out the consequence, because it is real and temporary:
`| UC-002-create-account | rejects a customer whose status is not ACTIVE | KYC must pass before
an account exists | UC-004 (backlog) — until it ships, UC-002 returns 422 on every real call |`.

